#!/usr/bin/python3 -IS
"""ops-root: the one root path on a host that the door manages (DevOps plan, section 2, "The host login").

The second sshd lets only the door's key in, as `ops-agent`, and its forced command (`ops-read`) gives the fixed reads
itself and passes every other form here, through one sudoers line:

    ops-agent ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/ops-root

    sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/ops-root <base64 of {"v": 1, "op": <form>, "args": [<text>, ...], "sha256": <hex>}>

Standard library only, the same file on every host, and its own check of each form: the door classifies a call with this
very table (door_targets.py loads this file), and this file refuses on its own what the door should never have sent, so
a door bug passes nothing that the table refuses. Stdin is closed except for a file's bytes, which must have the
request's SHA-256; there is no TTY and the environment is empty. A root-owned mode file, written at install, says `fix`
or `rule-only`: on the hub, a Fleet machine and a CI host that two businesses share it says `rule-only`, and then only
rule forms run here: the agent's own forms, the file install among them, are refused. An `exec` command runs in a
transient service that hides the host's app trees, the Docker and containerd sockets and its secret paths; a package
install and a file install run outside it. A write holds the host's one lock, so a second write waits. A new version of
this file comes only from a release that the release key signed (`self-update`), never a held one, and never one before
the release it came from.

Exit status: 0 done, 3 refused (the one line says why), 1 failed.
"""
